# String Matching Algorithms
# String matching is the process of finding one string (called the pattern) inside another string (called the text). It checks if the pattern exists in the text and, if so, finds its position(s). This task is fundamental in areas like search engines, DNA sequencing, and text editors. For example, searching for "abc" in "xabcyz" is a string matching problem. The goal is to find all occurrences efficiently.

# There are many ways to do this, starting with the naive approach, which tries every position and checks character by character. However, it can be slow for large inputs. More efficient algorithms like KMP, Rabin-Karp, Z-algorithm use preprocessing and pattern structure to reduce comparisons. These improve performance in worst-case scenarios.

